WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.370
- Hello, my name is Jorge Bañuelos.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.100
I'm a second year PhD
student in Yale's Department

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:11.940
of Religious Studies

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.800
in the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.210
Likewise, I have the pleasure

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.830
of being this year's graduate coordinator

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.840
for Yale Center for the Study of Race,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.150
Indigeneity, and Transnational Migration.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.020
And matter of fact, the rhythm
center is what connected us.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.720
Wadada Leo Smith is a fall 2022

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.630
Mellon Arts and Practitioner
Fellow with the center.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.370
And I'm very pleased to introduce him.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.009
I mean, we're sitting
here with a living legend.

00:00:34.009 --> 00:00:35.010
(Jorge laughs)

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:35.880
I can't overstate.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.520
We're here with a truly
prolific avant-garde musician

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.920
who I have the honor and
privilege of speaking with.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.360
Smith is a composer,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.433
multi-instrumentalist and an arranger.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.810
Residencies, you've had
residencies at the new school,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.280
in Georgetown, for example.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.400
You were on the faculty
for Cal Arts, correct?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.233
- Yes.

00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:55.560
- Yeah, and you were there
for about 20 or so years?

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:56.393
- 22.

00:00:56.393 --> 00:00:57.270
- Honorary doctorate,

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.490
and then also faculty emeritus as well.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.390
- Yes.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.426
- And then also started
your own record label.

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:06.196
I think, by my count, over
what, about 50 albums?

00:01:06.196 --> 00:01:07.290
- I got over a hundred

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.990
last year.
- [Jorge] Woo.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.250
So my count was off?
- Yeah.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.360
- And then likewise,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.760
finalists for the 2013
Pulitzer Prize in music.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.030
Also 2016 recipient of
the Doris Duke Award.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.470
Wow.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.300
Man, so, let me just,
I guess I'll just start

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.133
by way of an anecdote.

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.690
So I first had the
privilege of meeting Wadada

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:25.523
earlier in the fall.

00:01:25.523 --> 00:01:28.710
You gave a lecture at Timothy
Dwight College on campus.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.210
Speaking beforehand on the lecture,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.560
I was supposed to run the clicker.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.910
I said, man, don't worry about it.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.560
We'll just improvise, it's jazz.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.230
He recoiled. (laughs)

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.870
Would you mind telling us in
a sense why the words jazz

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.540
and improvisation are not
words that are in your lexicon?

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.603
- Well, essentially, they
was not intended for music.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:50.800
And those terms was actually forced upon

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.018
African American artistic community

00:01:54.018 --> 00:01:56.793
as a term that defined what they did.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.810
One of the traditions that humans practice

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:01.800
all over the world,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.800
and it's rare to be denied
it is a naming process.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.460
Humans named their
location, their siblings,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.650
their notion of what they do in life.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.080
All kinds of naming processes go on.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.250
So when the people looked at the music

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.810
coming outta early New Orleans,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.170
which is actually the
southern cradle of this music,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.273
they tagged it as jazz and
that it had improvisation in it

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:33.810
and all that kind of a stuff.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.450
Well, improvisation entered the lexicon

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:41.250
about 300 and something BC, you see?

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:45.030
And it was dealing with theater.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.030
Had nothing to do with music, okay?

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.330
And the word jazz come
from a baseball term

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:55.080
where they try to explain
the kind of energy

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.510
used in the participation
of that ball game.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.300
- [Jorge] Yeah, yeah.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.660
- And so, why would
you call the music jazz

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.050
or why would you employ
the word improvisation

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.710
when they have no meaning
whatsoever to that music?

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.053
Secondly, if you look at the definition,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.770
improvisation means virtually nothing.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:17.850
It's a collection of words

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:22.320
that you can't even get a
clear meaning out of, you see?

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.023
To do something offhandedly
that don't mean nothing.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.540
So I reject anything that's forced upon me

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.480
or anything that's forced upon the people

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.640
that I'm associated with.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.193
And that's the clearest
way I can define it.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.760
- Well, let me ask you
a follow up on that.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.080
So I gave an introduction,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.300
but it's always more special
when someone tells us

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.470
in their own words who they are.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.630
So would you mind introducing yourself

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.480
and telling me who you are
and how you narrate yourself?

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.133
- Well, I'm a human being.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.940
My existing on the planet
has something to do

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.940
with my character of making art

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.743
and making art in the present.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.880
I'm a family person.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.063
I love family because that's
the basic unit of humanity.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.990
And I like thinking and
deep reflecting about stuff.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.670
Prizes and ceremonies and things like that

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:24.510
are very important,

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:29.454
but they have very little practical use

00:04:29.454 --> 00:04:31.890
other than the fact that
somebody gives you something

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.053
or they something, something, something.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.230
But the whole point is,

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.810
is that I think of myself
as a creative person

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.693
who is constantly looking
and striving to discover,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.020
not just who I am,

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.700
but to discover the
largest or larger picture

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.753
of what this human
experience can be, you see?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.653
So that's how I would describe myself.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.050
- Something along the lines of the blues

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.790
and the abstract truth.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.770
- Well, it could be a
way of thinking about it,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.840
but see, even the notion of the blues

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.740
actually distorts what one would be.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:14.573
Because

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.090
blues is a cultural

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.373
notion about our society.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.660
It's a psychological way
of presenting information

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.633
into the society, larger
part of the society.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.100
When we think about blues,

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.210
we always think about
this notion of 12 bars

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.630
or somebody singing about something sad.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.613
Well, none of that's true.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.800
You see?

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.540
John Lee Hooker, for example,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:58.290
he doesn't observe no 12
bar frame for the blues.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.150
He plays as long as he would

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.370
on any one of those supposedly chords

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.130
that are associated with the blues.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.680
And he changed whenever he wants to.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.800
But even that's superficial

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.130
because that has no real true meaning.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.130
That is that he doesn't
observe this formalized form

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.640
that people have gone accustomed to

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.770
that they identify with blues.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.870
So what I'm trying to tell you

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.320
is that blues has several categories.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.673
Like for example,

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:33.693
Robert Johnson.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.260
Robert Johnson gives a exposition

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.200
on a piece that's called, I
forget the title of the piece,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.973
but the lyrics go like this,

00:06:45.945 --> 00:06:50.523
"When I die, you can bury my
old body beside the highway.

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:53.973
"So when the Greyhound bus come along,

00:06:54.817 --> 00:06:56.517
"I can get up and take a ride."

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:02.193
Now, that seemed like a nice
kind of a collection of words,

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.000
but that's deep meaning in that.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.780
It says the same thing that
any philosopher from Nietzsche

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.283
to Pythagoras to whoever.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:20.130
It says the same thing about that life,

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.280
that one survives death.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.070
Because if his old body that's
buried beside the highway.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.440
can get up and take a
ride on the Greyhound bus,

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.790
which is a physical
characteristics of traveling,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.590
it means that that life

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:38.163
or that consciousness of
that person survived death.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.723
But he says it within the limits
of two and a half minutes.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.597
Not in a book that has 500
pages or 1000 pages in it

00:07:47.597 --> 00:07:51.146
that goes rambling on and
on and on and on and on.

00:07:51.146 --> 00:07:54.300
It's one of the most succinct definitions

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.533
about this idea of
consciousness surpassing death.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.500
So that's one quality.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.495
John Lee Hooker has blues,
it's called "Tupelo Flood."

00:08:06.495 --> 00:08:08.611
That's one we just got to talking about,

00:08:08.611 --> 00:08:10.593
that's a philosophical blues.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.890
And the other one, the "Tupelo Flood,"

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.860
he talks about is actually
event that happened.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.393
So it's a historical blues.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.630
And you can go on and
identify and identify

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.090
all the way down the line
all these characteristics

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.020
that are associated with
the blues, where in fact,

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:32.310
the person that's truly
receiving this information

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.680
would gleam those kinds of qualities

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.960
that I'm talking about about it.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.930
And not the fact that it has 12 bars

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:43.800
or 32 bars or 19 bars or
one and a half bars long,

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.590
or that it has these old kind of sadness

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.630
or something, something,
something, something.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:49.470
You see?

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.750
All that stuff is dust,

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.966
but the real humans is
supposed to look through dust.

00:08:54.966 --> 00:08:57.240
You see, because the dust
settles after a while.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.983
Gravity takes care of that.

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:02.040
- And there are a couple ideas.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.560
There are a lot of ideas
in what you just said.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.110
I think there's one of
them I do wanna hop onto

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.980
about the existential quality of it.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.973
That, in a sense, because to
me, I'm a musician as well.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.340
Part of, to me, with the appeal

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.930
of being someone who makes music,

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.360
but then also deeply
appreciates the art form

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.210
is the kind of immediate
arrest you can get out of it.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.096
And what you're saying
about that blues line

00:09:23.096 --> 00:09:24.990
that you mentioned that
there's kind of like

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.970
an immediate haptic quality to it.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.820
Like it reaches out and kind
of grabs you or touches you.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.200
Is that related to inspiration?

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.043
Like, how?

00:09:33.161 --> 00:09:36.643
- That's a form of of
being, how can you say it?

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.969
Informed, it's more
like an informed notion

00:09:41.969 --> 00:09:45.090
where you actually are able to capture

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:48.000
the information that's hidden in the line,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.403
in a poetic line, you see?

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.420
And then secondly, that
kind of activity is called,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:09:59.700
you know, you discover.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.700
You're digging underneath
the lyrics to find the truth

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.670
of what's being spoken about, you see?

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:10.560
So yeah, you could say
that it does inspire you,

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.560
or one, to look deeper
each time for something,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:21.390
but the notion of inspiration
that we normally talk about

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.130
and associated with art

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:28.080
has to do with this idea that
new information is received,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.920
and it's received, if
you create a musician,

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.340
it's received in the present moment

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.630
while the activity of making
that art piece is happening.

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:41.190
So the ensemble is playing

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.470
and somebody who's in the
ensemble gets inspired,

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.900
and that inspiration is new information

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.910
that's put into the pool.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.020
And that delivers something different

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:54.497
than something that's
formalized and just practiced

00:10:54.497 --> 00:10:58.320
and delivered as what it is.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.820
And I don't mean to say that
a piece that's all written

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.540
cannot be, cannot carry inspiration.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.670
It can, okay?

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.640
Many artists, Duke Ellington
included have pieces

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.373
that are completely written out.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:16.200
Okay?

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:21.200
And they also have come from inspiration

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:26.070
and have this triggering
quality when it's performed

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:31.070
to inspire or be a source of
inspiration for the performer.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:36.780
Same thing could be said about a composer.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.820
Like, let's say, let's use an old guy

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.330
like Bach or Beethoven.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.700
Their music is all written out.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.020
Okay?

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.753
But that music came from inspiration.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:54.650
And it also, it's performing
can reach a level of creativity

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.550
where it also, to perform
it in that certain way,

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.223
they have to also receive
inspiration how to do it.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:06.780
- Receive inspiration.

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:09.750
So like, not necessarily generate
or come up with your own,

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:10.890
but it seems like the inspiration

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.427
is coming from somewhere else.

00:12:12.427 --> 00:12:13.800
- Yes, yes.
- Do you have a,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.390
could you put words, in a sense,

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:18.750
to what inspiration is and
where it might come from?

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:19.920
And where does your inspiration come from?

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.280
- Well, all inspiration
come from the same place.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.113
And it's unknown.

00:12:24.113 --> 00:12:25.990
It's called the unknown or the unseen.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:27.713
And

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:33.980
the idea of inspiration
means to be inspired

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:37.890
or to be input, something is input.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.980
And to describe it, it's like a stream

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.180
that flows through you
only for a few moments.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.250
You can't even calculate
it in terms of seconds,

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:48.083
it's a few moments.

00:12:48.083 --> 00:12:49.530
It flows through you.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.690
And once it passes through you,

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.180
it leaves it's imprint there.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.693
And what's imprinted there
is just for that moment.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:02.040
And if it's used in a
performance of any kind,

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.730
whether it be dancing, painting, writing,

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.690
thinking, or whatever,
it flows right through

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:12.210
and it comes out for that moment.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:13.043
You see?

00:13:13.043 --> 00:13:15.090
But that moment is like a spark

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.163
in a world that's completely dark.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.440
That spark lasts forever

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.600
because what was created from it

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.470
is gonna be part of the
human dimension forever.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.360
Both in terms of
actuality as a work of art

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.143
or as a memory.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:40.230
Those things are gonna
constantly be reincarnated

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.630
or reinvented through the population

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.700
of those that experience it.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.970
So the seed is this big from the moment

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.250
of actually expression,

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:52.500
it gets larger and larger and larger.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.180
And if somebody writes about it,

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:55.380
that's another source

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.494
where it is actually being
spreaded out, you see?

00:13:58.494 --> 00:14:01.863
So it actually has
something that it gives,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.730
both in a physical sense
and also a mental sense

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.430
where it actually continuously to live.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:13.430
And I may remind you that
nothing that's in creation

00:14:16.678 --> 00:14:19.667
is here and did not just
now come into creation.

00:14:19.667 --> 00:14:20.973
It was always here.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:25.413
And that which is here can never leave.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:30.240
I'm paraphrasing basically
the law of thermodynamics.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:31.073
You see?

00:14:31.073 --> 00:14:32.013
It can never leave.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:37.593
Spiritual texts state the
same thing in a different way,

00:14:39.035 --> 00:14:42.960
but it never leaves, you see?

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:43.793
Yeah.

00:14:43.793 --> 00:14:45.240
- So in a sense, it kind of sounds like

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.610
there's an absolute or eternal,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.410
and then a contraction of that

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.000
into the world that we live in.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.300
And then if we orient ourselves,
if we practice for it,

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.130
we might be lucky enough
to become a medium

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.780
for it to continue and move forward.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:58.920
- Yeah, yeah.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:02.880
The practice of it is
a quite simple thing.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.050
That is, one have to
be desired and in need

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:08.760
and in the search of it.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.598
And then when it does happen,

00:15:10.598 --> 00:15:14.373
one needs to be capable
of using it, you see?

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:17.700
Everybody's inspired.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.310
Most people don't know
what inspiration mean.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.860
They don't know what it feels
like inside of themselves.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:26.350
Mostly people who perform artistic works

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:30.630
recognize it more than ordinary people.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:35.580
And that's because they
are constantly searching

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:38.040
and striving to discover something.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:39.420
You see?

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.723
Ordinary people have these
same kinds of inspiration.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:45.870
It comes into them the same way,

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.963
but they don't pay attention to it often.

00:15:50.079 --> 00:15:53.389
They call it like daydreaming of fantasy

00:15:53.389 --> 00:15:57.300
or some vague idea or something.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.603
And they don't act on it,
they just keep moving past it.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.360
But it comes through everybody

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:06.240
because if a person is
being actually inspired

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.220
and you're standing close to them,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.203
some of that wave hits you as well.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:12.390
Okay?

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:13.800
Yeah.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:17.040
- So in relation to, because
you're a multi-instrumentalist,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.320
and I assume that you still
practice down to these days.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.450
- Yeah, yeah, I still practice.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.970
I won't say how much,
but I still a practice.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.650
- Yeah, 'cause I'm also asking too,

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:27.810
'cause for myself, I'm a drummer.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:29.400
I've noticed that practicing nowadays

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.170
has moved a lot towards technique

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:33.420
and also making sure that
when inspiration comes,

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:35.640
I'm actually kind of ready for the idea

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.770
to manifest in that moment.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.405
In a sense, how do you practice?

00:16:39.405 --> 00:16:41.700
How do you teach practicing,

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.260
and then how do you
personally approach it?

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:45.420
- Yeah, actually practicing
part little bit later,

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:47.340
but let me say this.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.740
Let me talk about technique.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.390
We've been taught that technique

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.438
is something that's taught to you.

00:16:53.438 --> 00:16:55.800
That's not quite true.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.950
Technique is the way
that you do something.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:00.810
Every time you go out of your house

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.423
and unlock the door or
come in and lock the door,

00:17:05.863 --> 00:17:09.840
that's a practical way that
you get into your doorway.

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:11.100
That's a technique.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.410
That's how you do it.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:18.410
Same thing with the way in
which you do your research.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:20.873
You see?

00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:23.790
Or how you take a walk.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:28.790
All these things are ways
and means towards something.

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:32.640
And when we talk about artistic technique,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.030
it's always implies that
it has been established

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:39.360
and endorsed by some kind of a teacher

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:41.820
or somebody or somebody something.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:44.700
And what I'm trying to
say is that technique

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:48.900
is actually the way that you
do something, how you do it,

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.980
and the skills with you
are able to do it with.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:53.970
You see?

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:56.760
So, how do you gain that technique?

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:00.090
You gain that technique
by allowing yourself

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:03.870
to be deeply aware of what you're doing

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.990
and to take note of it mentally

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:11.270
or otherwise of how one does it

00:18:13.637 --> 00:18:15.480
or how it's done.

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:16.313
You see?

00:18:16.313 --> 00:18:19.183
And that began to be part of your kit

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.687
of which you work through and work with.

00:18:25.687 --> 00:18:26.943
Okay?

00:18:26.943 --> 00:18:27.860
Practicing,

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:32.883
a lot of people practice from,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:37.230
you know, how their teacher
taught them to practice.

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.360
Lot of people practice

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.633
from how you think about
practice should be.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:49.323
I say find out how you learn
how to practice by practicing.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:55.260
And the way to do that
is not to fix anything

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:58.350
about what it is, how you practice.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:02.070
For drummers, there's a
different set of things

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:02.910
that you practice.

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:05.580
For wind instruments, there
are different set of things.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.250
Same thing for strings and so on.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:09.083
Okay?

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:11.493
For drumming,

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.553
everybody wants to be impressive.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.330
So they practice stuff
that would make them

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:23.375
appear to be impressive.

00:19:23.375 --> 00:19:25.890
That everybody can say, wow, that's that,

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:27.180
that's that's that.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:28.013
No.

00:19:31.222 --> 00:19:33.250
There are drummers out there

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:41.130
that play with what appears to be

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:43.263
no trained technique whatsoever.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:47.980
And of course they make the
largest impact in the world.

00:19:49.208 --> 00:19:52.503
There are drummers who
were extremely trained,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.970
but are able to overpass their training

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:01.830
and actually do something
that's absolutely phenomenal.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:04.263
That's a Tony Williams, for example.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.563
The other early drum I
was thinking about was,

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:13.200
we call him Big Chief, I'm
trying to think of his name.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:15.443
He played with Albert
Allen and those people.

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:18.273
Sunny Murray.

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:20.940
Sunny Murray plays the drums

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:25.940
like nobody you've ever
heard before, okay?

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:31.400
And his impact is tremendous.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:33.330
It's tremendous.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:34.320
Okay?

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:38.370
I mean, like the difference between him,

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:43.080
he's a new set of Baby Dodd as
Baby Dodd was in the twenties

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.283
to everybody else.

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:46.133
You see?

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:50.040
So that's the distinction.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:55.040
So, what I'm telling you this
is that if one is in a school,

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:00.690
you gotta find a way to discover

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:04.560
that real part of what you're
doing and why you're there.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.990
And if you're not in a school,

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.980
you still gotta find the real
part of what you're doing

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:11.813
and why you are there.

00:21:11.813 --> 00:21:13.983
That is, why you are in creation.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:17.850
And once you discover even a part of that,

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.070
you've come close to where you're at.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:23.313
That means you come close
to realizing your own state.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.200
You see?

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.030
And what is that own state?

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:28.290
That's a true state.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:33.290
I mean, it's called getting
back to your original self.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:34.710
You know?

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:35.543
Yeah.

00:21:35.543 --> 00:21:37.500
- Reminds me of this
quote by Ralph Ellison,

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:38.820
the author of "Invisible Man."

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:39.997
One of my favorite quotes where he says,

00:21:39.997 --> 00:21:42.247
"Good fiction is made up a reality,

00:21:42.247 --> 00:21:43.887
"but reality is hard to come by."

00:21:43.887 --> 00:21:46.415
(Jorge laughs)
- Well, it's true, yeah.

00:21:46.415 --> 00:21:48.060
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:49.440
- Because it also sounds like too,

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.620
in the style of practice or technique,

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:55.830
you know, just performing,
getting the ideas out

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:58.950
that also it's training
consciousness in a sense too

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:00.780
that you become aware of technique,

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:02.280
you become aware of
some things that you do

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:03.840
that kind of seem as if
they're just floating

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:04.890
in the background.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:06.690
So like, for example,

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:08.880
drummers, they tell you
practice with a metronome.

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.130
You're not getting it,
slow the metronome down.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:13.260
Moving slower gives you
more of an opportunity

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:15.180
to kind of hear the fine grains

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.980
of where certain beats are laid out.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.410
But it's also training
the mind, in a sense,

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:22.110
to be able to hear, to
perceive, to have better,

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:23.670
more accurate perception of sounds.

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:26.100
Also, in a sense, to be able to perceive

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.360
what is really real.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.190
- Yeah, that's one way.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:30.854
The other way is to just feel it.

00:22:30.854 --> 00:22:33.407
(Jorge laughs)

00:22:33.407 --> 00:22:37.320
Because a stroke, once you make a stroke,

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:39.780
it's already set up something.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:44.780
And what comes after that,
something comes after that.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:47.040
And the feeling tells you,

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:51.630
it nurtures you to stroke
again in a certain way.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:55.350
And if it tells you to
stroke more than one time,

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.990
and all these things are faster than,

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:02.763
they're almost faster
than reasoning or logic.

00:23:03.727 --> 00:23:08.400
So you're working from your
intuitive self, that stroking.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:10.383
And that becomes the,

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.460
that's a far more powerful
key than a metronome

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.350
because just you gotta
remember, Baby Dodd,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:22.157
they didn't have no metronomes, you know?

00:23:22.157 --> 00:23:23.370
- [Jorge] They didn't.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:24.203
- You see?

00:23:24.203 --> 00:23:28.110
What they understood was is
that if you initiate something,

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:30.990
that's the beginning and
that beginning opens up

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:33.360
all kinds of other possibilities.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:34.830
And that other possibility,

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.380
if he's drumming or she's drumming,

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:39.960
it's the notion of how it feels

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.850
that the next stroke comes in.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.770
And like I say, this stuff
is faster than lightning.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:47.910
You don't have to sit there
and tabulate it and add it up.

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:51.600
It's already, it has
gone 50,000 strokes away

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.498
from that adding up and
tabulation of it, you know?

00:23:55.498 --> 00:24:00.498
So yeah, metronomes can
be a by source, you know,

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:05.310
but also what overrides the
metronome is your feeling.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:06.510
Yeah.

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:08.310
- Because then going back to
the idea you were talking about

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:10.950
with some drummers or musicians in general

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:14.130
who don't necessarily adhere
to a school or it's style

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.520
who are not trying to have
that kind of discipline,

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:19.860
at least, to be disciplined by something

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.210
or someone beyond themselves,

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:23.670
but it's a matter of groove pocket.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:24.503
Does it feel good?

00:24:24.503 --> 00:24:25.920
Is it hitting how it should?

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:27.480
- Well, don't call it
groove, just call it feeling

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.780
because groove implies some
historical reference to a beat.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.820
And it's not about a beat

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:34.710
because a beat is about the count.

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.270
And it's not about the count.

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.340
That's what I'm saying,
it's about feeling.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:39.990
It's more about feeling.

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:43.053
I mean, a guy like a Max Roach,

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:50.840
they cannot throw out
those unusual accents

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:56.180
that's a 34th on the
end of a set of figures.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:59.190
They can't throw it out if
they're trying to count.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:01.350
It can only be done by feeling.

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:02.183
You see?

00:25:02.183 --> 00:25:03.300
And then when people try to tabulate,

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:05.850
they would need a machine to tabulate it.

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:06.780
You see?

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:11.160
The reason why is because the count,

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:14.760
which is always about
metrics that's not in.

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:17.103
There are other kinds
of rhythms out there.

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:20.610
My music explores those as well.

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:23.400
But they're called proportional.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:27.417
They're not based off of a metrical cycle.

00:25:27.417 --> 00:25:31.930
Metric cycle has either
12 strokes in it divided

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.670
or four strokes in it divided,

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:37.710
or two strokes in it divided.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:39.240
That's all metrics.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:40.590
And there's a limit to it.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:42.330
If you look at the Indian system

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.760
where they have have
this horizontal string

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:48.720
of numbers thrown out,
you add them all up,

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.880
and if they come out to even number,

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:53.130
then you divide it up evenly

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:56.580
and you can say that
it's 25000 four-fourths.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:57.413
Okay?

00:25:57.413 --> 00:26:01.293
Or if it's uneven, you can
say it's 5000 four-fourths,

00:26:02.213 --> 00:26:06.270
2000 three-fourths, 1000 one-fourth.

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:07.103
You see?

00:26:07.103 --> 00:26:08.430
But still all metric.

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:11.850
Proportion deals with long
and short relationship.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:13.530
- And you get rid of the fixity too.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:16.800
- Yeah, you get rid of
the fixed quality of it

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.920
is that it's either long
or is it either short.

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:22.560
And it alternates between those.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.240
Long, long, long, short,

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:26.430
or short, long, short, short, short,

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.680
long, short, long, short, long.

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:29.700
That's not gonna come up.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:33.540
You can't even count that
up in terms of these markers

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:37.200
I'm talking about metrically
because the impetus,

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:39.870
just like the idea of feeling,

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:42.810
it's been pulled away from the metrics.

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:44.760
And there there are only
two kinds of rhythm.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:47.520
Those are metrical rhythms

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:50.370
and those which are
based off of proportion.

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:51.240
Yeah.

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:52.073
- Man, I don't know.

00:26:52.073 --> 00:26:52.906
I'm gonna have to disagree with you.

00:26:52.906 --> 00:26:56.523
I like the fixed beat,
but maybe that's just me.

00:26:57.402 --> 00:27:00.570
- But liking is overrated.

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:01.403
You see?

00:27:01.403 --> 00:27:03.060
Liking is based off of-
- I need some model.

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:06.390
- Liking is based off a lot of expectation

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:07.920
and stuff like that.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.920
But you know, you can like stuff,

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:14.973
but when the lights go out, dislike it.

00:27:16.071 --> 00:27:17.868
(Jorge laughs)

00:27:17.868 --> 00:27:19.950
- Hey, so this is a good
segue to move into something

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:21.120
that I forgot to mention earlier,

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.250
which is you have your own language

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:24.480
for doing musical notation.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:25.950
Would you speak a little bit about that?

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:27.660
- Yeah, it's called Ankh Ross Nation.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:28.493
- [Jorge] Ankh Ross Nation.

00:27:28.493 --> 00:27:29.940
- Ankh Ross Nation.
- [Jorge] So like, Ankh,

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:31.140
Ross, Nation.
- Nation, yes.

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:33.000
- [Jorge] Got you.
- And Ankh, of course,

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:38.000
is the Egyptian cross, which
means vital life force,

00:27:38.603 --> 00:27:42.210
basically, because when you
look at the temple drawings,

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:44.250
they pointed towards the head,

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.080
they pointed towards the mouth and nose

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.340
because that's the breath,

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:49.830
they pointed towards
the neck and the chest,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:52.290
and sometimes they pointed
over top of the head.

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:53.460
Okay?

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:56.149
So Ankh means vital life force.

00:27:56.149 --> 00:27:59.400
And it's a cross that's like that.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:00.233
Okay?

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.950
Ross come from the Amharic,

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.170
which is the ancient Egyptian language.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:08.943
I mean, Ethiopian language.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:11.640
It mainly come from these,

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:16.640
but we have it rendered in
Amharic, and Ross means head.

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:21.060
Which infers the father

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:23.550
because we talking about a family unit.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.250
And Mars is the mother's son.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:29.400
So if you look at Ankh Ross Nation,

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:33.423
it means the vital life
force, the mother and father.

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:37.390
Which is the total

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:42.180
gamut in which reproduction

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:45.240
and creativity is taking place through.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:47.730
So it's not named after
some musical thing.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:51.240
It's named after the actual
source of regeneration

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:55.770
that takes place between the
idea of mother and father.

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:56.603
You see?

00:28:56.603 --> 00:28:59.913
So when you look at that,
it's a symbolic language.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:06.560
And by symbolic meaning that
it has figures, or symbols,

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:11.133
it has color.

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:16.140
Everything that's connected
with it has to be referenced

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:18.390
or put in place by the person

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.540
that's making the
observation of his score.

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:22.253
And

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:28.383
it's a score that's also based off of

00:29:28.383 --> 00:29:30.363
the notion of regeneration.

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:35.460
The velocity units, which deals with stuff

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.473
that has a lot of activity in it.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:41.640
Has four different density levels,

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:45.213
that is levels of thickness and et cetera.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.300
That's on the fast side.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:53.300
On the slow side, it has four
levels of lessen of density.

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:55.860
It begins to, from one to four,

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:58.443
the density level begin to decrease.

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:01.078
And so there's, again,

00:30:01.078 --> 00:30:04.890
this idea of the right
and left, long and short.

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:06.300
And then the rhythm units,

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.670
there are six sets of
those, actually seven,

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:10.653
but I only use six nowadays.

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:14.591
They also have this notion
of long short, long short,

00:30:14.591 --> 00:30:17.310
long short, based off of six sets.

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:18.143
Okay?

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:26.752
The point I'm trying to
make is that the language

00:30:26.752 --> 00:30:31.752
was discovered in 1967
on a Saturday morning.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:38.030
And it's still growing in,

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:39.993
what is it, 2022?

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:42.420
You see?

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:46.950
Because as I was saying that
when you really get deep

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:49.140
into language, and
particularly symbolic language,

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:52.530
every object that we see around us,

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:56.700
inside or outside of building,
all of that is language.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.650
All of it, all of it.

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:01.290
And all of it came from inspiration.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:02.790
You see?

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:05.100
That camera wasn't
constructed by some person.

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:08.670
They wanted to construct something
because they had an idea.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:12.753
No, they were inspired to
look for how to do that.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:15.360
- So two more questions.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:18.360
